window.AUTH=window.AUTH||{};window.AUTH["18-217"]={"d":"18-217","id":"2019/18-217","n":"Mathena v. Malvo","briefs":[{"k":"petitioner","label":"Brief of petitioner Randall Mathena","date":"2019-06-11","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/DocketPDF/18/18-217/102632/20190611133404376_2018%2006%2011%20Brief%20for%20Petitioner.pdf","side":"pet","n":91},{"k":"united-states","label":"Brief amicus curiae of United States","date":"2019-06-18","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/DocketPDF/18/18-217/103282/20190618125743345_18-217tsacUnitedStates.pdf","side":"us","n":33},{"k":"respondent","label":"Brief of respondent Lee Boyd Malvo","date":"2019-08-20","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/DocketPDF/18/18-217/112991/20190820182546105_18-217%20bs.pdf","side":"res","n":32},{"k":"reply","label":"Reply of petitioner Randall Mathena","date":"2019-09-19","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/DocketPDF/18/18-217/116384/20190919150941730_2019%2009%2019%20Reply%20Brief.pdf","side":"pet","n":23}],"opinion":null,"tr":true,"items":[{"t":"case","label":"Arizona v. Roberson","cite":"486 U.S. 675","y":1988,"id":"1987/87-354","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that once a suspect has requested counsel, police cannot interrogate him unless he initiates the contact.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"17"}},{"t":"case","label":"Armour & Co. v. Wantock","cite":"323 U.S. 126","y":1944,"id":"scdb/1944-014","sc":1,"sum":"1. Fireguards employed by a manufacturer of goods for interstate comnerce, held covered by the Fair Labor Standards Act of 1938, as employed in an \"occupation necessary to the production\" of goods for interstate commerce.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"33"}},{"t":"case","label":"Beard v. Banks","cite":"542 U.S. 406","y":2004,"id":"2003/02-1603","sc":1,"sum":"In a 5-to-4 decision written by Justice Clarence Thomas, the Court found that the rule announced in Mills - that sentencing schemes could not prevent jurors from considering mitigating evidence that had not been accepted unanimously when deciding whether to apply the death penalty - was a new rule, because it was not compelled by previous Court decisions.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"17","1":"27","2":"45"}},{"t":"case","label":"Brecht v. Abrahamson","cite":"507 U.S. 619","y":1993,"id":"1992/91-7358","sc":1,"sum":"Chief Justice William H. Rehnquist wrote that the \"substantial and injurious effect\" test was preferable to the \"beyond a reasonable doubt\" test in requests for habeas corpus relief. Habeas proceedings are not to be the primary avenue for resolving disputes; Rehnquist called the writ \"an extraordinary remedy\", reserved for the victims of grave injustice.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"8"}},{"t":"case","label":"Bucklew v. Precythe","cite":"139 S. Ct. 1112","y":2019,"id":"2018/17-8151","sc":1,"sum":"Baze v. Rees, 553 U. S. 35, and Glossip v. Gross, 576 U. S. ___, govern all Eighth Amendment challenges alleging that a method of execution inflicts unconstitutionally cruel pain; petitioner’s as-applied challenge to Missouri’s single-drug execution protocol—that it would cause him severe pain because of his particular medical condition—fails to satisfy the Baze-Glossip test.","sumSrc":"Holding (supremecourt.gov)","by":{"0":"25"}},{"t":"case","label":"Butler v. McKellar","cite":"494 U.S. 407","y":1990,"id":"1989/88-6677","sc":1,"ar":1,"sum":"Six weeks after Pamela Lane was murdered, petitioner Butler was arrested on an unrelated assault charge for which he retained counsel. While in custody, Butler was informed that he was a suspect in Lane's murder. After receiving Mirandawarnings, he signed waiver of rights forms and made incriminating statements about the Lane murder during interrogation. At his capital murder trial, the court denied his motion to …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"17"}},{"t":"case","label":"Calderon v. Coleman","cite":"525 U.S. 141","y":1998,"id":"1998/98-437","sc":1,"sum":"In a 5-4 per curiam opinion, the Court held that \"once the Court of Appeals determined that the giving of the Briggs instruction was constitutional error, it was bound to apply the harmless-error analysis mandated by Brecht.\" The Court held in Brecht that \"a federal court may grant habeas relief based on trial error only when that error \"'had substantial and injurious effect or influence in determining the jury's verdict.'\" Dissenting, Justice John Paul Stevens wrote that \"there might have been a slight flaw in the Court of Appeals' brief explanation of why the invalid instruction given to the jury was not harmless, but...the Court's ruling was unquestionably correct.\" Justices David H. Souter, Ruth Bader Ginsburg, and Stephen G. Breyer joined Justice Stevens' dissent.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"13","3":"18"}},{"t":"case","label":"Caldwell v. Mississippi","cite":"472 U.S. 320","y":1985,"id":"1984/83-6607","sc":1,"sum":"The death sentence is vacated. 443 So. 2d 806, reversed in part and remanded.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"17"}},{"t":"case","label":"California v. San Pablo & T.R. Co.","cite":"149 U.S. 308","y":1893,"id":"scdb/1892-221","sc":1,"sum":"If, pending a writ of error to reverse a judgment for the defendant in an action by a State to recover sums of money for taxes, the defendant offers to the plaintiff, and deposits in a bank to its credit, the amount of those-sums, with penalties, interest and costs, which by a statute of the State have the same effect as actual payment and receipt of the money, the-writ of error must be dismissed.","sumSrc":"Headnote (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"31"}},{"t":"case","label":"Caspari v. Bohlen","cite":"510 U.S. 383","y":1994,"id":"1993/92-1500","sc":1,"sum":"The state trial judge sentenced respondent as a persistent offender following his conviction on three robbery counts, but the Missouri Court of Appeals reversed the sentence because there was no proof of prior convictions, as is necessary to establish persistent-offender status under state law. On remand, the trial judge resentenced respondent as a persistent offender based on evidence of prior felony convictions, …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"17"}},{"t":"case","label":"Chaidez v. United States","cite":"568 U.S. 342","y":2013,"id":"2012/11-820","sc":1,"sum":"The Court found that the Padilla rule created an entirely new rule regarding ineffective assistance of counsel and therefore did not apply to persons whose conviction became final before its announcement.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"17"}},{"t":"case","label":"Clay v. United States","cite":"537 U.S. 522","y":2003,"id":"2002/01-1500","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that, for the purpose of starting the clock on section 2255's one-year limitation period, a judgment of conviction becomes final when the time expires for filing a petition for certiorari contesting the appellate court's affirmation of the conviction. Under this rule, Clay's section 2255 motion was timely filed.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"12"}},{"t":"case","label":"Clemons v. Mississippi","cite":"494 U.S. 738","y":1990,"id":"1989/88-6873","sc":1,"sum":"At the sentencing hearing following petitioner Clemons' Mississippi capital murder conviction, the trial court instructed the jury, among other things, that, in deciding whether to impose the death penalty, it should consider the following statutory aggravating factors: (1) that the murder was committed during the course of a \"robbery for pecuniary gain,\" and (2) that it was an \"'especially heinous, atrocious or …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"17"}},{"t":"case","label":"Crawford v. Washington","cite":"541 U.S. 36","y":2004,"id":"2003/02-9410","sc":1,"sum":"The Court sided with Crawford and ruled that the Sixth Amendment's Confrontation Clause gives defendants the right to confront witnesses and cross-examine their testimony. This includes testimony police gather. The Court reasoned that the Framers intended the Confrontation Clause to prohibit out-of-court testimony as evidence against defendants.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"17"}},{"t":"case","label":"Cutter v. Wilkinson","cite":"544 U.S. 709","y":2005,"id":"2004/03-9877","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that, on its face, RLUIPA made an accommodation allowed by the First Amendment. The Court reasoned that the law was an effort to alleviate the \"government-created burden\" on religious exercise that prisoners faced. Nor did section three discriminate between mainstream and non-mainstream religions.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"24"}},{"t":"case","label":"Danforth v. Minnesota","cite":"552 U.S. 264","y":2008,"id":"2007/06-8273","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that the federal case law precedent, including Teague did not constrain the authority of state courts to give broader effect to new rules of criminal procedure than was required by the precedent. The precedent applied only to federal habeas corpus petitions and had no bearing on state post-conviction proceedings.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"14, 15, 29"}},{"t":"case","label":"Davis v. United States","cite":"564 U.S. 229","y":2011,"id":"2010/09-11328","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that the good-faith exception to the exclusionary rule applies to a search that was authorized by precedent at the time of the search, but was subsequently ruled unconstitutional.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"14"}},{"t":"case","label":"Desist v. United States","cite":"394 U.S. 244","y":1969,"id":"1968/12","sc":1,"sum":"The decision in Katz v. United States, 389 U. S. 347, which held that the reach of the Fourth Amendment \"cannot turn upon the presence or absence of a physical intrusion into any given enclosure,\" and that every electronic eavesdropping upon private conversations is a search and seizure which, as a general rule, can comply with constitutional standards only when authorized by a magistrate on a showing of probable …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"14"}},{"t":"case","label":"Dodd v. United States","cite":"545 U.S. 353","y":2005,"id":"2004/04-5286","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that the text of the federal law \"unequivocally\" stated that the one-year limitation period begins to run on the date on which the Court \"initially recongized\" the right, not the date on which that right was made retroactive.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"29, 30"}},{"t":"case","label":"Duncan v. Commonwealth","cite":"343 S.E.2d 392","y":1986,"court":"Va. Ct. App.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/S.E.2d/343/392/","by":{"0":"5"}},{"t":"case","label":"Eddings v. Oklahoma","cite":"455 U.S. 104","y":1982,"id":"1981/80-5727","sc":1,"ar":1,"sum":"The Court held that the trial court erred when it failed to consider mitigating evidence of Eddings’ unhappy childhood and emotional disturbance.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"26","2":"25, 26, 34"}},{"t":"case","label":"Engle v. Isaac","cite":"456 U.S. 107","y":1982,"id":"1981/80-1430","sc":1,"sum":"Ruled in favor of Engle and stated that the respondents cannot challenge the constitutionality of the Ohio jury instructions in federal habeas corpus proceedings.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"16"}},{"t":"case","label":"Espinosa v. Florida","cite":"505 U.S. 1079","y":1992,"id":"1991/91-7390","sc":1,"sum":"If a weighing State requires a trial court to pay deference to a jury's sentencing recommendation in determining the appropriate sentence, the jury's consideration of an invalid aggravating circumstance unconstitutionally infects the court's sentencing determination. Instructions more specific and elaborate than the one given in the instant case have been found unconstitutionally vague, and the weighing of an …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"17"}},{"t":"case","label":"Gilmore v. Taylor","cite":"508 U.S. 333","y":1993,"id":"1992/91-1738","sc":1,"sum":"At his trial in Illinois state court, respondent Taylor admitted the killing with which he was charged, but presented evidence to support his claim that he was only guilty of voluntary manslaughter. The jury received instructions modeled after the state pattern instructions on murder and voluntary manslaughter and convicted Taylor of murder. After the conviction and sentence became final, he sought federal habeas …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"17"}},{"t":"case","label":"Goeke v. Branch","cite":"514 U.S. 115","y":1995,"id":"1994/94-898","sc":1,"sum":"The State did not waive the Teague issue, and application of the Eighth Circuit's novel rule violates Teague's holding. The record supports the State's position that it raised the Teague claim in the District Court and the Eighth Circuit. Thus, it must be considered now, and it is dispositive. See Caspariv. Bohlen, 510 U. S. 383, 389. The Eighth Circuit's fugitive dismissal rule was neither dictated nor compelled by …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"17"}},{"t":"case","label":"Graham v. Collins","cite":"506 U.S. 461","y":1993,"id":"1992/91-7580","sc":1,"sum":"A case in which the Court declined to create a new constitutional rule that would require special jury instructions to consider the mitigating circumstances of the defendant's youth and troubled family life.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"17","3":"12"}},{"t":"case","label":"Graham v. Florida","cite":"560 U.S. 48","y":2010,"id":"2009/08-7412","sc":1,"sum":"The Court found that a non-homicidal conviction leading to a life sentence without parole for a juvenile violates the Eighth Amendment.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"19, 25, 35","1":"14","2":"11, 23, 24"}},{"t":"case","label":"Gray v. Netherland","cite":"518 U.S. 152","y":1996,"id":"1995/95-6510","sc":1,"sum":"Announced by Chief Justice William H. Rehnquist, the Court ruled that the notice-of-evidence claim would require the adoption of a new constitutional rule and therefore could not be raised in a habeas petition.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"17"}},{"t":"case","label":"Greene v. Fisher","cite":"565 U.S. 34","y":2011,"id":"2011/10-637","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that decisions of the Court do not constitute \"clearly established federal law\" if the decision predates the decision of a relevant state court.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"13"}},{"t":"case","label":"Griffith v. Kentucky","cite":"479 U.S. 314","y":1987,"id":"1986/85-5221","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that after a new rule had been decided in a particular case, \"the integrity of judicial review requires that we apply that rule to all similar cases pending on direct review.\" The Court reasoned that selective application of new rules violated the principle of treating similarly situated defendants on an equal basis.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"8, 9, 15, 35"}},{"t":"case","label":"Harper v. Virginia Dep’t of Taxation","cite":"509 U.S. 86","y":1993,"id":"1992/91-794","sc":1,"sum":"In Davis v. Michigan Dept. of Treasury,489 U. S. 803, this Court invalidated Michigan's practice of taxing retirement benefits paid by the Federal Government while exempting retirement benefits paid by the State or its political subdivisions. Because Michigan conceded that a refund to federal retirees was the appropriate remedy, the Court remanded for entry of judgment against the State. Virginia subsequently …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"13"}},{"t":"case","label":"Henry v. City of Rock Hill","cite":"376 U.S. 776","y":1964,"id":"1963/826","sc":1,"sum":"Edwards and Fields, which established that the peaceful expression of unpopular views at a place not lawfully proscribed by state law is protected by the Fourteenth Amendment from state criminal action, are controlling here. Certiorari granted and judgment reversed. Jack Greenberg, Constance Baker Motley, Matthew J. Perry, Lincoln C. Jenkins, Jr., Donald James Sampson and Willie T. Smith, Jr. for petitioners.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"34"}},{"t":"case","label":"Jackson v. Hobbs","cite":"565 U.S. 1013","y":2011,"url":"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/565/1013/","sc":1,"by":{"0":"19, 19"}},{"t":"case","label":"Johnson v. Texas","cite":"509 U.S. 350","y":1993,"id":"1992/92-5653","sc":1,"sum":"Justice Anthony M. Kennedy wrote for the majority and held that the procedure set forth by the Texas capital sentencing statute was constitutional with respect to the Eighth and Fourteenth Amendments.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"27"}},{"t":"case","label":"Johnson v. United States","cite":"135 S. Ct. 2551","y":2015,"id":"2014/13-7120","sc":1,"sum":"The Court determined that the residual clause of the Armed Criminal Career Act is unconstitutionally vague.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"16"}},{"t":"case","label":"Jones v. Commonwealth","cite":"763 S.E.2d 823","y":2014,"court":"Va.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/S.E.2d/763/823/","by":{"0":"24","2":"14, 15","3":"16, 17, 18"}},{"t":"case","label":"Jones v. Commonwealth","cite":"795 S.E.2d 705","y":2017,"court":"Va.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/S.E.2d/795/705/","by":{"0":"6, 13, 24, 33","1":"7, 22, 30","2":"14, 15, 49, 51","3":"16"}},{"t":"case","label":"Kuhn v. Fairmont Coal Co.","cite":"215 U.S. 349","y":1910,"id":"scdb/1909-039","sc":1,"sum":"When administering state laws and determining rights accruing-thereunder, the jurisdiction of the Federal court is an independent-one, coordinate and concurrent with, and not subordinate to, the. jurisdiction of the state courts.","sumSrc":"Headnote (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"13"}},{"t":"case","label":"Lambrix v. Singletary","cite":"520 U.S. 518","y":1997,"id":"1996/96-5658","sc":1,"sum":"Authored by Justice Antonin Scalia, the Court ruled that a prisoner whose conviction became final before the ruling, which held that if the sentencing judge in a \"weighing\" State is required to give deference to a jury's advisory sentencing recommendation, then neither the jury nor the judge is constitutionally permitted to weigh invalid aggravating circumstances, is foreclosed from relying on that decision in a federal habeas corpus proceeding.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"17, 27","3":"3"}},{"t":"case","label":"Lawrence v. Chater","cite":"516 U.S. 163","y":1996,"id":"1995/94-9323","sc":1,"sum":"In a 7-2 per curiam opinion, the Court held that it had the power to issue a GVR order and that such an order is an appropriate exercise of its discretionary certiorari jurisdiction. \"In an appropriate case, a GVR order conserves the scarce resources of this Court that might otherwise be expended on plenary consideration, assists the court below by flagging a particular issue that it does not appear to have fully considered, assists this Court by procuring the benefit of the lower court's insight before we rule on the merits, and alleviates the 'potential for unequal treatment' that is inherent in our inability to grant plenary review of all pending cases raising similar issues,\" stated the unsigned opinion.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"33"}},{"t":"case","label":"Lockett v. Ohio","cite":"438 U.S. 586","y":1978,"id":"1977/76-6997","sc":1,"ar":1,"sum":"The Court held that the Eighth and Fourteenth Amendments required, in all but the rarest capital cases, that sentencers not be precluded from considering a range of mitigating factors before imposing the death penalty. These factors included any aspect of a defendant's character or record and any circumstances of the offense proffered as a reason for a sentence less than death.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"26"}},{"t":"case","label":"Linkletter v. Walker","cite":"381 U.S. 618","y":1965,"id":"1964/95","sc":1,"sum":"In a 7-to-2 decision, the Court held that the Constitution neither prohibited nor required \"an absolute rule of retroaction.\" Only a case-by-case examination of the rules in question was required. The Court reasoned that police misconduct prior to Mapp had already occurred and would not be corrected by releasing the prisoners involved.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"13, 14"}},{"t":"case","label":"Mackey v. United States","cite":"401 U.S. 667","y":1971,"id":"1970/36","sc":1,"sum":"At petitioner's trial for, income tax evasion, the Gavernment used monthly wagering tax forms petitioner had filed, as required by statutei to show that the gross -amount of wagers he reported, less business expenses, exceeded the gambling profits reported on his income tax returns. Petitioner objected on the ground that-the forms were prejudicial and irrelevant, but he was convicted in 1964 and the Court of Appeals …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"14, 15, 17","2":"39"}},{"t":"case","label":"Malvo v. Mathena","cite":"893 F.3d 265","y":2018,"court":"4th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/893/265/","by":{"0":"1"}},{"t":"case","label":"Malvo v. Mathena","cite":"254 F. Supp. 3d 820","y":2017,"court":"E.D. Va.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.%20Supp.%203d/254/820/","by":{"0":"1"}},{"t":"case","label":"Mapp v. Ohio","cite":"367 U.S. 643","y":1961,"id":"1960/236","sc":1,"sum":"The Court decided that evidence obtained illegally may not be used against someone in a court of law by the Fourth Amendment.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"14"}},{"t":"case","label":"Maynard v. Cartright","cite":"486 U.S. 356","y":1988,"id":"1987/87-519","sc":1,"sum":"As applied in this case, the statutory aggravating circumstance was unconstitutionally vague.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"17"}},{"t":"case","label":"Miller v. Alabama","cite":"567 U.S. 460","y":2012,"id":"2011/10-9646","sc":1,"ar":1,"sum":"The Court found it to be a violation of the Eighth Amendment to impose a sentence of life imprisonment without the possibility of parole on juvenile offenders.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"passim, 19, 19, 28","1":"passim","2":"passim","3":"passim"}},{"t":"case","label":"Mills v. Maryland","cite":"486 U.S. 367","y":1988,"id":"1987/87-5367","sc":1,"sum":"Petitioner, a Maryland prison inmate, was tried by a state-court jury and convicted of the first-degree murder of his cellmate. In the trial's sentencing phase, the same jury found that the State had established the statutory aggravating factor that petitioner committed the murder while he was confined in a correctional institution, and marked \"no\" beside each mitigating circumstance referenced on the verdict form, …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"17"}},{"t":"case","label":"Montgomery v. Louisiana","cite":"136 S. Ct. 718","y":2016,"id":"2015/14-280","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that the Supreme Court’s decision in Miller v. Alabama, which prohibits sentencing schemes that impose a punishment of mandatory life without parole for juvenile offenders convicted of homicide, applied retroactively.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"passim","1":"passim","2":"passim","3":"passim"}},{"t":"case","label":"Muhammad v. Commonwealth","cite":"619 S.E.2d 16","y":2005,"court":"Va.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/S.E.2d/619/16/","by":{"0":"3","1":"2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 9","2":"8"}},{"t":"case","label":"Muhammad v. Kelly","cite":"575 F.3d 359","y":2009,"court":"4th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/575/359/","by":{"0":"3"}},{"t":"case","label":"Muhammad v. State","cite":"934 A.2d 1059","y":2007,"court":"Md. Ct. Spec. App.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/A.2d/934/1059/","by":{"0":"2, 3","1":"3, 4, 5, 6","2":"5, 8, 10"}},{"t":"case","label":"O’Dell v. Netherland","cite":"521 U.S. 151","y":1997,"id":"1996/96-6867","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that the rule was new, and that it could not be used to disturb O'Dell's death sentence, which had been final for six years when Simmons was decided. Justice Thomas reasoned that the rule was new within the meaning of Teague v Lane, 489 U.S. 288.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"17","1":"27","2":"45"}},{"t":"case","label":"Padilla v. Kentucky","cite":"559 U.S. 356","y":2010,"id":"2009/08-651","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that counsel must inform a client whether or not his plea carries a risk of deportation under the Sixth Amendment.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"17"}},{"t":"case","label":"Penry v. Lynaugh","cite":"492 U.S. 302","y":1989,"id":"1988/87-6177","sc":1,"sum":"The Court partially affirmed and partially reversed the lower court's decision. Justice O'Connor argued that the jury was improperly instructed and should have been told that it could have considered Penry's mental deficiencies when imposing its sentence.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"15, 16, 30, 30","1":"17, 18","2":"28, 29, 35, 36, 38"}},{"t":"case","label":"Pickelsimer v. Wainwright","cite":"375 U.S. 2","y":1963,"id":"1963/16_misc","sc":1,"sum":"Certiorari granted; judgments vacated; and cases remanded for further consideration in light of Gideon v. Wainwright, 372 U. S. 335. Petitioners pro se. Richard W. Ervin, Attorney General of Florida, and A. G. Spicola,Jr., Assistant Attorney General, for respondent in No. 16, Misc., No. 60, Misc., and No. 70, Misc. Richard W. Ervin, Attorney General of Florida, and George R. Georgieff, Assistant Attorney General, …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"13"}},{"t":"case","label":"Rankin v. Commonwealth","cite":"825 S.E.2d 81","y":2019,"court":"Va.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/S.E.2d/825/81/","sum":"Stating that “[t]he jury recommended a sentence of two years and six months’ imprisonment, and the circuit court sentenced Rankin in accordance with the jury’s recommendation”.","sumSrc":"brief:0","by":{"0":"5"}},{"t":"case","label":"Reiter v. Sonotone Corp.","cite":"442 U.S. 330","y":1979,"id":"1978/78-690","sc":1,"sum":"Consumers who pay a higher price for goods purchased for personal use as a result of antitrust violations sustain an injury in their \"property\" within the meaning of § 4.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"33"}},{"t":"case","label":"Ring v. Arizona","cite":"536 U.S. 584","y":2002,"id":"2001/01-488","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that, because Arizona's enumerated aggravating factors operates as \"the functional equivalent of an element of a greater offense,\" the Sixth Amendment requires that they be found by a jury.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"17"}},{"t":"case","label":"Roberts v. Louisiana","cite":"428 U.S. 326","y":1976,"id":"1975/75-5844","sc":1,"sum":"The Supreme Court also held that by requiring jurors to be instructed on the lesser charges of manslaughter and second-degree murder, even if no evidence exits to support such verdicts, Louisiana's sentencing scheme encourages them to disregard their oaths by recommending a verdict for a lesser offense whenever they feel that the death penalty is inappropriate.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"26"}},{"t":"case","label":"Roberts v. Louisiana","cite":"431 U.S. 633","y":1977,"id":"1976/76-5206","sc":1,"sum":"The mandatory death sentence unposed upon petitioner pursuant to a Louisiana statute for the first-degree murder of a police officer engaged m the performance of his lawful duties held to violate the Eighth and Fourteenth Amendments, since the statute allows for no consideration of particularized mitigating factors n deciding whether the death sentence should be imposed. 331 So. 2d 11, reversed and remanded.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"26"}},{"t":"case","label":"Robinson v. Neil","cite":"409 U.S. 505","y":1973,"id":"1972/71-6272","sc":1,"sum":"Waller v. Florida, 397 U. S. 387, which bars on the ground of double jeopardy two prosecutions, state and municipal, based on the same act or offense, is fully retroactive. Pp. 506-511. 452 F. 2d 370, vacated and remanded.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"13"}},{"t":"case","label":"Roper v. Simmons","cite":"543 U.S. 551","y":2005,"id":"2004/03-633","sc":1,"ar":6,"sum":"The Court held that executing minors under the death penalty is unconstitutional.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"4, 18, 25, 35","1":"14","2":"11, 21, 22, 23"}},{"t":"case","label":"Saffle v. Parks","cite":"494 U.S. 484","y":1990,"id":"1989/88-1264","sc":1,"sum":"Parks is not entitled to federal habeas relief. The principle he urges is a \"new rule\" of federal constitutional law that can neither be announced nor applied in a case on collateral review unless it comes within one of two narrow-and here inapplicable-exceptions. Teaqne v. Lane, 489 U. S. 288; Penry v. Lynaugh, 492 U. S. 302.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"11, 17, 28","1":"27","3":"3, 8"}},{"t":"case","label":"Sawyer v. Smith","cite":"497 U.S. 227","y":1990,"id":"1989/89-5809","sc":1,"sum":"Petitioner is not entitled to federal habeas relief, because Caldwell announced a new rule, as defined by Teague, that does not come within either of the Teague exceptions.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"17"}},{"t":"case","label":"Schriro v. Summerlin","cite":"542 U.S. 348","y":2004,"id":"2003/03-526","sc":1,"sum":"In a 5-to-4 decision written by Justice Antonin Scalia, the Court held that the changed announced in Ring v. Arizona was merely procedural and therefore not retroactively applicable.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"17, 26","1":"17, 23, 24, 27","2":"29, 36, 38","3":"9"}},{"t":"case","label":"Simmons v. South Carolina","cite":"512 U.S. 154","y":1994,"id":"1993/92-9059","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that a state court’s refusal to instruct a jury in a sentencing trial that a defendant is ineligible for parole violated the Due Process Clause of the Fourteenth Amendment.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"17"}},{"t":"case","label":"Stringer v. Black","cite":"503 U.S. 222","y":1992,"id":"1991/90-6616","sc":1,"sum":"In a federal habeas corpus proceeding, a petitioner whose death sentence became final before Maynard and Clemons were decided is not foreclosed by Teague from relying on those cases.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"17"}},{"t":"case","label":"Sumner v. Shuman","cite":"483 U.S. 66","y":1987,"id":"1986/86-246","sc":1,"sum":"While serving a life sentence without possibility of parole upon a firstdegree murder conviction, respondent was convicted of the capital murder of a fellow prisoner and sentenced to death under a Nevada statute mandating the death penalty in these circumstances. The State Supreme Court affirmed respondent's conviction and death sentence. The Federal District Court, in a habeas corpus proceeding, vacated the death …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"25"}},{"t":"case","label":"Tatum v. Arizona","cite":"137 S. Ct. 11","y":2016,"url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/S.%20Ct./137/11/","sc":1,"by":{"0":"33, 34"}},{"t":"case","label":"Teague v. Lane","cite":"489 U.S. 288","y":1989,"id":"1988/87-5259","sc":1,"ar":8,"sum":"The judgment is affirmed. 820 F. 2d 832, affirmed. JUSTICE O'CONNOR delivered the opinion of the Court with respect to Parts I, II, and III, concluding that:","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"passim","1":"17, 24","2":"16, 28, 39, 45, 53","3":"3"}},{"t":"case","label":"Welch v. United States","cite":"136 S. Ct. 1257","y":2016,"id":"2015/15-6418","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that the rule the Supreme Court announced in Johnson v. United States regarding what constitutes a violent offense for the purposes of sentencing under the Armed Career Criminal Act applies retroactively.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"8, 16","1":"29"}},{"t":"case","label":"Whorton v. Bockting","cite":"549 U.S. 406","y":2007,"id":"2006/05-595","sc":1,"sum":"The Court ruled unanimously \"that Crawford announced a 'new rule' of criminal procedure and that this rule does not fall within the Teague exception for watershed rules.\" Justice Samuel Alito's opinion called the Crawford ruling \"flatly inconsistent\" with the prior precedents that it overruled. It was therefore a new rule of criminal procedure.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"17"}},{"t":"case","label":"Woodson v. North Carolina","cite":"428 U.S. 280","y":1976,"id":"1975/75-5491","sc":1,"ar":2,"sum":"In a 5-to-4 decision, the Court held that the North Carolina law was unconstitutional. The Court found three problems with the law: First, the law \"depart[ed] markedly from contemporary standards\" concerning death sentences. The historical record indicated that the public had rejected mandatory death sentences.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"25, 26, 27","1":"14","2":"26, 34","3":"10, 11"}},{"t":"case","label":"Wright v. West","cite":"505 U.S. 277","y":1992,"id":"1991/91-542","sc":1,"sum":"The judgment is reversed, and the case is remanded. 931 F. 2d 262, reversed and remanded. JUSTICE THOMAS, joined by THE CMHEF JUSTICE and JUSTICE ScALiA, concluded that regardless of whether a federal habeas court should review state-court applications of law to fact deferentially or de novo, the trial record contains more than enough evidence to support West's conviction. Jackson repeatedly emphasizes the deference …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"16"}},{"t":"const","label":"Amendment V","clauses":[["grand-jury","Grand Jury Clause"],["double-jeopardy","Double Jeopardy Clause"],["self-incrimination","Self-Incrimination Clause"],["due-process-5","Due Process Clause (Fifth Amendment)"],["takings","Takings Clause"]],"text":"No person shall be held to answer for a capital, or otherwise infamous crime, unless on a presentment or indictment of a Grand Jury, except in cases arising in the land or naval forces, or in the Militia, when in actual service in time of War or public danger; nor shall any person be subject for the same offence to be twice put in jeopardy of life or limb; nor shall be compelled in any criminal case to be a witness against himself, nor be deprived of life, liberty, or property, without due process of law; nor shall private property be taken for public use, without just compensation.","by":{"0":"30"}},{"t":"const","label":"Amendment VIII","clauses":[["eighth-amendment","Eighth Amendment"]],"text":"Excessive bail shall not be required, nor excessive fines imposed, nor cruel and unusual punishments inflicted.","by":{"0":"passim","1":"2, 11, 13, 15, 20","2":"4","3":"passim"}},{"t":"const","label":"Amendment XIV","clauses":[["citizenship-clause","Citizenship Clause"],["privileges-or-immunities","Privileges or Immunities Clause"],["due-process-14","Due Process Clause (Fourteenth Amendment)"],["equal-protection","Equal Protection Clause"],["disqualification","Disqualification Clause (Section 3)"],["enforcement-14","Enforcement Clause (Section 5)"]],"by":{"0":"4, 26"}},{"t":"usc","label":"28 U.S.C. § 1254","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/28/1254","subs":["(1)"],"by":{"0":"2"}},{"t":"usc","label":"28 U.S.C. § 2244","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/28/2244","subs":["(d)(1)(C)","(d)(1)"],"stext":{"head":"§2244. Finality of determination","paras":[[0,"(a) No circuit or district judge shall be required to entertain an application for a writ of habeas corpus to inquire into the detention of a person pursuant to a judgment of a court of the United States if it appears that the legality of such detention has been determined by a judge or court of the United States on a prior application for a writ of habeas corpus, except as provided in section 2255."],[0,"(b)(1) A claim presented in a second or successive habeas corpus application under section 2254 that was presented in a prior application shall be dismissed."],[0,"(2) A claim presented in a second or successive habeas corpus application under section 2254 that was not presented in a prior application shall be dismissed unless—"],[1,"(A) the applicant shows that the claim relies on a new rule of constitutional law, made retroactive to cases on collateral review by the Supreme Court, that was previously unavailable; or"],[1,"(B)(i) the factual predicate for the claim could not have been discovered previously through the exercise of due diligence; and"],[1,"(ii) the facts underlying the claim, if proven and viewed in light of the evidence as a whole, would be sufficient to establish by clear and convincing evidence that, but for constitutional error, no reasonable factfinder would have found the applicant guilty of the underlying offense."],[0,"(3)(A) Before a second or successive application permitted by this section is filed in the district court, the applicant shall move in the appropriate court of appeals for an order authorizing the district court to consider the application."],[0,"(B) A motion in the court of appeals for an order authorizing the district court to consider a second or successive application shall be determined by a three-judge panel of the court of appeals."],[0,"(C) The court of appeals may authorize the filing of a second or successive application only if it determines that the application makes a prima facie showing that the application satisfies the requirements of this subsection."],[0,"(D) The court of appeals shall grant or deny the authorization to file a second or successive application not later than 30 days after the filing of the motion."],[0,"(E) The grant or denial of an authorization by a court of appeals to file a second or successive application shall not be appealable and shall not be the subject of a petition for rehearing or for a writ of certiorari."],[0,"(4) A district court shall dismiss any claim presented in a second or successive application that the court of appeals has authorized to be filed unless the applicant shows that the claim satisfies the requirements of this section."],[0,"(c) In a habeas corpus proceeding brought in behalf of a person in custody pursuant to the judgment of a State court, a prior judgment of the Supreme Court of the United States on an appeal or review by a writ of certiorari at the instance of the prisoner of the decision of such State court, shall be conclusive as to all issues of fact or law with respect to an asserted denial of a Federal right which constitutes ground for discharge in a habeas corpus proceeding, actually adjudicated by the Supreme Court therein, unless the applicant for the writ of habeas corpus shall plead and the court shall find the existence of a material and controlling fact which did not appear in the record of the proceeding in the Supreme Court and the court shall further find that the applicant for the writ of habeas corpus could not have caused such fact to appear in such record by the exercise of reasonable diligence."],[0,"(d)(1) A 1-year period of limitation shall apply to an application for a writ of habeas corpus by a person in custody pursuant to the judgment of a State court. The limitation period shall run from the latest of—"],[1,"(A) the date on which the judgment became final by the conclusion of direct review or the expiration of the time for seeking such review;"],[1,"(B) the date on which the impediment to filing an application created by State action in violation of the Constitution or laws of the United States is removed, if the applicant was prevented from filing by such State action;"],[1,"(C) the date on which the constitutional right asserted was initially recognized by the Supreme Court, if the right has been newly recognized by the Supreme Court and made retroactively applicable to cases on collateral review; or"],[1,"(D) the date on which the factual predicate of the claim or claims presented could have been discovered through the exercise of due diligence."],[0,"(2) The time during which a properly filed application for State post-conviction or other collateral review with respect to the pertinent judgment or claim is pending shall not be counted toward any period of limitation under this subsection."]],"more":false,"ed":"2024","src":"https://www.govinfo.gov/link/uscode/28/2244?type=usc&year=mostrecent&link-type=html"},"head":"§2244. Finality of determination","tname":"Judiciary and Judicial Procedure","by":{"0":"29","1":"9","2":"53"}},{"t":"usc","label":"28 U.S.C. § 2254","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/28/2254","subs":["(d)"],"stext":{"head":"§2254. State custody; remedies in Federal courts","paras":[[0,"(a) The Supreme Court, a Justice thereof, a circuit judge, or a district court shall entertain an application for a writ of habeas corpus in behalf of a person in custody pursuant to the judgment of a State court only on the ground that he is in custody in violation of the Constitution or laws or treaties of the United States."],[0,"(b)(1) An application for a writ of habeas corpus on behalf of a person in custody pursuant to the judgment of a State court shall not be granted unless it appears that—"],[1,"(A) the applicant has exhausted the remedies available in the courts of the State; or"],[1,"(B)(i) there is an absence of available State corrective process; or"],[1,"(ii) circumstances exist that render such process ineffective to protect the rights of the applicant."],[0,"(2) An application for a writ of habeas corpus may be denied on the merits, notwithstanding the failure of the applicant to exhaust the remedies available in the courts of the State."],[0,"(3) A State shall not be deemed to have waived the exhaustion requirement or be estopped from reliance upon the requirement unless the State, through counsel, expressly waives the requirement."],[0,"(c) An applicant shall not be deemed to have exhausted the remedies available in the courts of the State, within the meaning of this section, if he has the right under the law of the State to raise, by any available procedure, the question presented."],[0,"(d) An application for a writ of habeas corpus on behalf of a person in custody pursuant to the judgment of a State court shall not be granted with respect to any claim that was adjudicated on the merits in State court proceedings unless the adjudication of the claim—"],[1,"(1) resulted in a decision that was contrary to, or involved an unreasonable application of, clearly established Federal law, as determined by the Supreme Court of the United States; or"],[1,"(2) resulted in a decision that was based on an unreasonable determination of the facts in light of the evidence presented in the State court proceeding."],[0,"(e)(1) In a proceeding instituted by an application for a writ of habeas corpus by a person in custody pursuant to the judgment of a State court, a determination of a factual issue made by a State court shall be presumed to be correct. The applicant shall have the burden of rebutting the presumption of correctness by clear and convincing evidence."],[0,"(2) If the applicant has failed to develop the factual basis of a claim in State court proceedings, the court shall not hold an evidentiary hearing on the claim unless the applicant shows that—"],[1,"(A) the claim relies on—"],[2,"(i) a new rule of constitutional law, made retroactive to cases on collateral review by the Supreme Court, that was previously unavailable; or"],[2,"(ii) a factual predicate that could not have been previously discovered through the exercise of due diligence; and"],[1,"(B) the facts underlying the claim would be sufficient to establish by clear and convincing evidence that but for constitutional error, no reasonable factfinder would have found the applicant guilty of the underlying offense."],[0,"(f) If the applicant challenges the sufficiency of the evidence adduced in such State court proceeding to support the State court's determination of a factual issue made therein, the applicant, if able, shall produce that part of the record pertinent to a determination of the sufficiency of the evidence to support such determination. If the applicant, because of indigency or other reason is unable to produce such part of the record, then the State shall produce such part of the record and the Federal court shall direct the State to do so by order directed to an appropriate State official. If the State cannot provide such pertinent part of the record, then the court shall determine under the existing facts and circumstances what weight shall be given to the State court's factual determination."],[0,"(g) A copy of the official records of the State court, duly certified by the clerk of such court to be a true and correct copy of a finding, judicial opinion, or other reliable written indicia showing such a factual determination by the State court shall be admissible in the Federal court proceeding."],[0,"(h) Except as provided in section 408 of the Controlled Substances Act, in all proceedings brought under this section, and any subsequent proceedings on review, the court may appoint counsel for an applicant who is or becomes financially unable to afford counsel, except as provided by a rule promulgated by the Supreme Court pursuant to statutory authority. Appointment of counsel under this section shall be governed by section 3006A of title 18."],[0,"(i) The ineffectiveness or incompetence of counsel during Federal or State collateral post-conviction proceedings shall not be a ground for relief in a proceeding arising under section 2254."]],"more":false,"ed":"2024","src":"https://www.govinfo.gov/link/uscode/28/2254?type=usc&year=mostrecent&link-type=html"},"head":"§2254. State custody; remedies in Federal courts","tname":"Judiciary and Judicial Procedure","by":{"0":"13","1":"9"}},{"t":"usc","label":"28 U.S.C. § 2255","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/28/2255","by":{"0":"29"}},{"t":"rule","label":"Fed. R. Civ. P. 15","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/rules/frcp/rule_15","by":{"0":"29"}},{"t":"case","label":"Petition for Writ of Certiorari, Montgomery v. Louisiana","cite":"136 S. Ct. 718","y":2016,"url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/S.%20Ct./136/718/","sc":1,"sum":"Describing it as “unlikely that, after holding mandatory death penalty statutes unconstitutional, the Court would have denied collateral relief on non-retroactivity grounds for a capital defendant who never had an opportunity to argue for a sentence less than death”.","sumSrc":"brief:0","by":{"0":"22, 30, 31, 26"}},{"t":"case","label":"Arkansas Game & Fish Comm’n v. United States","cite":"568 U.S. 23","y":2012,"id":"2012/11-597","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held the Fifth Amendment's Taking Clause could be enacted when a temporary flood regime destroyed property.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"22","3":"5, 6"}},{"t":"case","label":"Bousley v. United States","cite":"523 U.S. 614","y":1998,"id":"1997/96-8516","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that, although Bousley's claim was procedurally defaulted, Bousley may be entitled to a hearing on the merits of it if he makes the necessary showing to relieve the default.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"24, 25"}},{"t":"case","label":"Buchanan v. Angelone","cite":"522 U.S. 269","y":1998,"id":"1997/96-8400","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that the trial court's failure to instruct the jury on the concept of mitigation generally and on the four mitigating factors did not violate Buchanan's rights under the Eighth and Fourteenth Amendments.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"28"}},{"t":"case","label":"Gaylor v. United States","cite":"74 F.3d 214","y":1996,"url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/74/214/","by":{"1":"21"}},{"t":"case","label":"Landgraf v. USI Film Prods.","cite":"511 U.S. 244","y":1994,"id":"1993/92-757","sc":1,"sum":"Section 102 does not apply to a Title VII case that was pending on appeal when the 1991 Act was enacted.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"1":"22"}},{"t":"case","label":"Malvo v. Mathena","cite":"259 F. Supp. 3d 321","y":2017,"court":"D. Md.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.%20Supp.%203d/259/321/","by":{"1":"8, 9"}},{"t":"case","label":"North Carolina v. Alford","cite":"400 U.S. 25","y":1970,"id":"1970/14","sc":1,"sum":"The Supreme Court held that there was no constitutional violation where the defendant concluded based on competent counsel that it was in his best interest to plead guilty. The court saw no difference between a defendant who maintains his innocence with a plea bargain and one who admits to the crime.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"8","2":"10"}},{"t":"case","label":"People v. Davis","cite":"6 N.E.3d 709","y":2014,"url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/N.E.3d/6/709/","sum":"Recognizing that “Miller mandates a sentencing range broader than that provided by statute” for certain juvenile offenders.","sumSrc":"brief:1","by":{"1":"29"}},{"t":"case","label":"People v. Holman","cite":"91 N.E.3d 849","y":2017,"court":"Ill.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/N.E.3d/91/849/","by":{"1":"21"}},{"t":"case","label":"State v. Mantich","cite":"842 N.W.2d 716","y":2014,"url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/N.W.2d/842/716/","sum":"Recognizing that “Miller required Nebraska to change its substantive punishment for the crime of first degree murder when committed by a juvenile”.","sumSrc":"brief:1","by":{"1":"29"}},{"t":"case","label":"State v. Montgomery","cite":"242 So. 2d 818","y":1970,"court":"La.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/So.%202d/242/818/","by":{"1":"18"}},{"t":"case","label":"Steilman v. Michael","cite":"407 P.3d 313","y":2017,"court":"Mont.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/P.3d/407/313/","by":{"1":"22"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Fareed","cite":"296 F.3d 243","y":2002,"url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/296/243/","sum":"Explaining that federal courts of appeals are “bound by Supreme Court dicta almost as firmly as by the Court’s outright holdings”.","sumSrc":"brief:1","by":{"1":"21"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Stitt","cite":"139 S. Ct. 399","y":2018,"id":"2018/17-765","sc":1,"sum":"The term “burglary” in the Armed Career Criminal Act includes burglary of a structure or vehicle that has been adapted or is customarily used for overnight accommodation.","sumSrc":"Holding (supremecourt.gov)","by":{"1":"30"}},{"t":"case","label":"Yakus v. United States","cite":"321 U.S. 414","y":1944,"id":"1940-1955/321us414","sc":1,"sum":"The Supreme Court held that Congress sufficiently regulated the powers of the Administrator of the Act in such a way as to be a constitutional delegation of legislative power to obtain a particular objective. The Constitution grants Congress the necessary flexibility to perform its legislative function in a variety of ways.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"30"}},{"t":"case","label":"County of Allegheny v. ACLU","cite":"492 U.S. 573","y":1999,"id":"1988/87-2050","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that depending on its \"particular physical setting,\" some displays of religious celebrations on government property were a violation of the Establishment Clause of the First Amendment.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"2":"40"}},{"t":"case","label":"Hamilton v. Director of the Department of Corrections","cite":"2014 Va. LEXIS 201","y":2014,"court":"Va. June 6","by":{"2":"51"}},{"t":"case","label":"Harris v. Commonwealth","cite":"688 S.E.2d 279","y":2010,"court":"Va.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/S.E.2d/688/279/","by":{"2":"51"}},{"t":"case","label":"Jefferson v. Commonwealth","cite":"2013 WL 5801746","y":2013,"court":"Va. Ct. App. Oct. 29","by":{"2":"51"}},{"t":"case","label":"Jones v. Virginia","cite":"136 S. Ct. 1358","y":2016,"url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/S.%20Ct./136/1358/","sc":1,"sum":"Observing that “the majority’s initial description of Miller is the same as our own”.","sumSrc":"brief:2","by":{"2":"14","3":"16"}},{"t":"case","label":"Mejia-Velez v. United States","cite":"320 F. Supp. 3d 496","y":2018,"court":"E.D.N.Y.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.%20Supp.%203d/320/496/","ar":1,"by":{"2":"21, 42"}},{"t":"case","label":"Moore v. Hinkle","cite":"527 S.E.2d 419","y":2000,"court":"Va.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/S.E.2d/527/419/","by":{"2":"51"}},{"t":"case","label":"Seminole Tribe of Florida v. Florida","cite":"517 U.S. 44","y":1996,"id":"1995/94-12","sc":1,"sum":"In a 5-to-4 decision, The Court held that Congress did intend to abrogate states' sovereign immunity under the IGRA but that the Indian Commerce Clause (and by implication the Commerce Clause) did not give Congress that power. Under the Eleventh Amendment, all states are regarded as sovereign entities.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"2":"33, 40"}},{"t":"case","label":"Shalala v. Illinois Council on Long Term Care, Inc.","cite":"529 U.S. 1","y":2000,"id":"1999/98-1109","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that the Medicare Act barred federal-question jurisdiction of the Council's challenges to the validity of various Medicare regulations. The Court concluded that the Council must proceed instead through the special review channel that the Medicare statutes create.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"2":"40"}},{"t":"case","label":"State v. Malvo","cite":"2017 WL 3579711","y":2017,"court":"Md. Cir. Ct. June 15","by":{"2":"10","3":"8, 14"}},{"t":"case","label":"Tate v. Showboat Marina Casino Partnership","cite":"431 F.3d 580","y":2005,"court":"7th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/431/580/","by":{"2":"34"}},{"t":"case","label":"Tyson v. Commonwealth","cite":"2015 WL 10945037","y":2015,"court":"Va. Aug. 14","by":{"2":"51"}},{"t":"case","label":"White v. Commonwealth","cite":"1997 WL 583578","y":1997,"court":"Va. Ct. App. Sept. 23","by":{"2":"51"}},{"t":"case","label":"Bouie v. City of Columbia","cite":"378 U.S. 347","y":1964,"id":"1963/10","sc":1,"sum":"The State Supreme Court, in giving retroactive application to its new construction of the statute, has deprived petitioners of their right to fair warning of a criminal prohibition, and thus has violated the Due Process Clause of the Fourteenth Amendment. Pp. 348-363. 239 S. C. 570, 124 S. E. 2d 332, reversed.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"3":"18"}},{"t":"case","label":"Cohens v. Virginia","cite":"19 U.S. 264","y":1821,"id":"1789-1850/19us264","sc":1,"sum":"The Court decided it had jurisdiction over all cases involving Constitutional questions, including state supreme court decisions.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"3":"4, 5, 6"}},{"t":"case","label":"Gregg v. Georgia","cite":"428 U.S. 153","y":1976,"id":"1975/74-6257","sc":1,"sum":"The Court ruled that the death penalty was not \"cruel and unusual\" punishment in violation of the Eighth and Fourteenth Amendments.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"3":"11"}},{"t":"case","label":"Jurek v. Texas","cite":"428 U.S. 262","y":1976,"id":"1975/75-5394","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that the death penalty was not per se a \"cruel and unusual\" punishment. Furthermore, the capital sentencing procedure in Texas was not unconstitutional on the theory that it would result in arbitrary and freakish impositions of the death penalty.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"3":"11"}},{"t":"case","label":"Marbury v. Madison","cite":"5 U.S. 137","y":1803,"id":"1789-1850/5us137","sc":1,"sum":"A case in which the Court established a precedent for judicial review in the United States, declaring that acts of Congress that conflict with the Constitution are null and void, as the Constitution is the supreme law of the land.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"3":"4"}},{"t":"case","label":"McCray v. Illinois","cite":"386 U.S. 300","y":1967,"id":"1966/159","sc":1,"sum":"Following receipt of information from an informer, two Chicago policemen made a warrantless arrest of the petitioner for possessing narcotics. At the pretrial hearing on petitioner's motion to suppress the evidence which was found on his person, the officers testified that: the informant had told them that petitioner \"was selling narcotics and had narcotics on his person\" and the area where petitioner could then be …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"3":"5"}},{"t":"case","label":"Proffitt v. Florida","cite":"428 U.S. 242","y":1976,"id":"1975/75-5706","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that the death penalty was not a \"cruel and unusual\" punishment per se, and that Florida's capital-sentencing procedure was not unconstitutionally arbitrary and/or capricious. Although empowering trial judges with sole sentencing authority, the statutory procedure tightly prescribed their relevant decision-making process.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"3":"11"}},{"t":"case","label":"Rita v. United States","cite":"551 U.S. 338","y":2007,"id":"2006/06-5754","sc":1,"sum":"The Court ruled that courts of appeals may apply a nonbinding presumption of reasonableness to within-Guidelines sentences. Justice Stephen Breyer wrote the opinion for the 8-1 majority.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"3":"13"}}]};